::ENG2_U05_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best combines these ideas? "The library closes early. Students lose a quiet place to study."{
=A. Because the library closes early, students lose a quiet place to study.#Correct. This shows the cause-and-effect relationship clearly.
~B. Although the library closes early, students lose a quiet place to study.#Incorrect. Although suggests contrast, not cause.
~C. The library closes early, students lose a quiet place to study.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~D. Library closes students quiet.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which transition shows contrast?{
~A. therefore#Incorrect. Therefore shows result.
=B. however#Correct. However shows contrast.
~C. for example#Incorrect. This introduces an example.
~D. in addition#Incorrect. This adds information.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which transition shows addition?{
~A. however#Incorrect. This shows contrast.
~B. therefore#Incorrect. This shows result.
=C. in addition#Correct. This adds another related idea.
~D. although#Incorrect. This introduces contrast.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is correctly punctuated?{
~A. The library closes early, students wait in hallways.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~B. The library closes early students wait in hallways.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~C. Because the library closes early.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=D. The library closes early; as a result, students wait in hallways.#Correct. This correctly joins related complete thoughts.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which transition best fits? "The library has limited staffing; _____, students still need a supervised place to study."{
=A. however#Correct. However signals contrast between limited staffing and continuing student need.
~B. therefore#Incorrect. Therefore would show result.
~C. for example#Incorrect. This introduces an example.
~D. in addition#Incorrect. This adds information but does not show contrast.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which paragraph is most cohesive?{
~A. The library closes. Buses. Hallway. Work.#Incorrect. This is fragmented.
=B. Because the library closes before late buses depart, students often wait in hallways. As a result, they lose access to a quiet place to complete assignments.#Correct. The sentences connect logically with cause and result.
~C. The library closes and however therefore students.#Incorrect. The transitions are misused.
~D. Students wait. They wait. They wait.#Incorrect. This is repetitive and choppy.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence fixes the run-on? "Students ride late buses they need a place to study."{
~A. Students ride late buses they need a place to study.#Incorrect. This keeps the run-on.
~B. Students ride late buses, they need a place to study.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
=C. Students ride late buses, so they need a place to study.#Correct. So correctly joins the related ideas.
~D. Because students ride late buses.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which transition best introduces an example?{
~A. however#Incorrect. This shows contrast.
~B. therefore#Incorrect. This shows result.
~C. although#Incorrect. This introduces contrast.
=D. for example#Correct. For example introduces a specific example.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word choice is most precise for a formal audience?{
=A. supervised study space#Correct. This phrase is precise and formal.
~B. good place#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. cool spot#Incorrect. This is informal.
~D. thing for work#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence best shows result?{
~A. The library closes early; however, students work in the hallway.#Incorrect. This suggests contrast.
=B. The library closes early; therefore, some students work in the hallway.#Correct. Therefore shows result.
~C. The library closes early; for example, students work in the hallway.#Incorrect. This introduces an example, not the main result.
~D. Although the library closes early, students work in the hallway.#Incorrect. Although suggests contrast.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is a fragment?{
~A. Students need a quiet place after school.#Incorrect. This is complete.
~B. The library closes before late buses depart.#Incorrect. This is complete.
=C. Because students need a quiet place after school.#Correct. This dependent clause is incomplete by itself.
~D. Extended hours would support students.#Incorrect. This is complete.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best reduces choppiness?{
~A. Students wait. Hallways loud. Work hard.#Incorrect. This is still choppy and unclear.
~B. Library open good students work.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~C. Students need it. It is good. They wait.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
=D. Students who wait for late buses need a quiet place to complete assignments after school.#Correct. This combines related ideas clearly.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which transition would best connect an additional reason?{
=A. In addition#Correct. In addition signals another related point.
~B. However#Incorrect. However signals contrast.
~C. Although#Incorrect. Although introduces contrast.
~D. As a result#Incorrect. This signals an effect.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence uses a comma correctly after an introductory clause?{
~A. Because students ride late buses they need a quiet place.#Incorrect. A comma is needed after the introductory clause.
=B. Because students ride late buses, they need a quiet place.#Correct. The comma follows the introductory clause.
~C. Because, students ride late buses they need a quiet place.#Incorrect. The comma is misplaced.
~D. Students, ride late buses because they need a quiet place.#Incorrect. The comma separates subject and verb.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which transition is most precise for a complication? "The library has limited staff. _____, students still need a safe place to wait."{
~A. Therefore#Incorrect. This shows result.
~B. In addition#Incorrect. This adds information.
=C. However#Correct. However shows the complication or contrast.
~D. For example#Incorrect. This introduces an example.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best supports audience clarity?{
~A. Use random transitions to sound advanced.#Incorrect. Transitions must match meaning.
~B. Make every sentence very long.#Incorrect. Length does not guarantee clarity.
~C. Remove all transitions.#Incorrect. Transitions help show relationships.
=D. Use transitions that accurately show cause, contrast, addition, or result.#Correct. Accurate transitions support audience understanding.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence correctly combines the ideas? "The hallways are loud. The library is quiet."{
=A. The hallways are loud, but the library is quiet.#Correct. But shows contrast and correctly joins the ideas.
~B. The hallways are loud, the library is quiet.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~C. The hallways loud library quiet.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~D. Because the hallways are loud.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which transition best shows cause?{
~A. However#Incorrect. This shows contrast.
~B. In addition#Incorrect. This adds information.
~C. For example#Incorrect. This introduces an example.
=D. Because#Correct. Because introduces a reason or cause.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word has the most formal tone?{
~A. stuff#Incorrect. This is informal.
~B. cool#Incorrect. This is informal.
=C. support#Correct. Support is precise and formal in this context.
~D. awesome#Incorrect. This is informal.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence best explains a transition choice?{
~A. I used however because it sounded fancy.#Incorrect. This does not connect transition to meaning.
=B. I used as a result because the second sentence explains what happens after the library closes early.#Correct. This explains the relationship accurately.
~C. I used because randomly.#Incorrect. Transitions should not be random.
~D. I removed the transition so readers can guess.#Incorrect. The goal is clarity.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence avoids a comma splice?{
=A. Students wait in hallways, so they need a quiet study space.#Correct. So correctly joins the complete thoughts.
~B. Students wait in hallways, they need a quiet study space.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~C. Students wait in hallways they need a quiet study space.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~D. Because students wait in hallways.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which transition would best connect this pair? "The library offers a quiet study area. It also provides computers for assignments."{
~A. However#Incorrect. This suggests contrast.
=B. In addition#Correct. The second sentence adds another related benefit.
~C. Although#Incorrect. This suggests contrast.
~D. Therefore#Incorrect. This suggests result.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence uses precise wording and clear flow?{
~A. It helps and is good and stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. Library things help people.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. Extended hours would provide students with supervised time to complete assignments.#Correct. This is precise and cohesive.
~D. Good things happen at the place.#Incorrect. This is vague.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence uses although correctly?{
~A. Although students need a place after school.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~B. Although the library, limited staff students.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~C. Although, students need place after school library.#Incorrect. This is unclear and incorrectly punctuated.
=D. Although the library has limited staff, students still need a supervised place after school.#Correct. Although introduces a contrast or complication.
}

::ENG2_U05_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which checklist best matches Lesson 6 revision?{
=A. Identify idea relationships, choose accurate transitions, control sentence length, check punctuation, and reread for audience clarity.#Correct. This is the Lesson 6 revision process.
~B. Add transitions randomly and make every sentence longer.#Incorrect. Transitions must match meaning.
~C. Remove punctuation and hope readers understand.#Incorrect. Conventions support clarity.
~D. Use informal words to make the paragraph sound casual.#Incorrect. Audience-aware revision requires appropriate tone.
}
